  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Sun Jun 28 2026\n\nToday through Saturday...As they gradually slide northeast and east \nof the Lower Mississippi River Valley, high pressure at both the \nsurface and aloft will maintain a presence across the forecast area \nthis week. This will provide some opportunity for the CWA to dry out \nover at least the next couple days.  However the trade-off of having \nthese dry days, will be that of a rather hot and humid stretch of \nweather existing across the region throughout this forecast period. \nCurrently, the next best chance for rain will come Tuesday, with \nchances remaining in the forecast through week's end, as disturbances \ncaught up in easterly flow aloft on the southern periphery of \nthe aforementioned high aloft, affect the forecast area.\n\nIn the meantime, increasing heat stress will be the primary story \nduring this forecast stretch.  This afternoon, highs will warm into \nthe low and middle 90s, but will increase to the mid and upper 90s \nMonday through the remainder of the period. This, coupled with dew \npoints in the 70s, will yield afternoon heat index values above the \ncentury mark throughout the forecast period.  At the moment, heat \nindices look their highest Monday and onward as values could reach \nup to around 110F. However come Tuesday, and again continuing \nthrough Saturday, increased rain chances each afternoon could have \nan affect on just how high heat index readings get.  I'll continue \nto advertise these dangerous heat stress conditions in the HWO this \nweek. We will also evaluate on a daily basis whether or not, at a \nminimum, a \"Heat Advisory\" is needed for portions of the forecast \narea.  Currently, a \"Heat Advisory\" in effect for mainly western and \nsouthern portions of the area from noon today through 9 PM Monday. \nThis advisory will likely need to be expanded eastward in the coming \ndays. /19/",
